1
00:00:04,002 --> 00:00:09,007
Your side of the swap. You hold shielded ZEC and want bitcoin. No one in between.

2
00:00:09,007 --> 00:00:15,007
The other side locks their bitcoin first, in a Taproot output.

3
00:00:15,007 --> 00:00:21,007
Your ZEC goes in shielded, to an address only this swap can open.

4
00:00:21,007 --> 00:00:27,007
You claim the bitcoin with one signature, and that hands them their key to the ZEC.

5
00:00:27,007 --> 00:00:33,007
Done. To anyone watching, an ordinary bitcoin payment.

6
00:00:33,007 --> 00:00:39,563
Underneath, the other side read z from your signature, and with z and b swept the shielded note.

7
00:00:39,563 --> 00:00:47,567
What each chain saw: on Bitcoin, one-signature Taproot payments; on Zcash, ciphertext. Nothing links them.

8
00:00:47,567 --> 00:00:50,970
And if the other side vanishes?

9
00:00:50,970 --> 00:00:56,970
A second swap: bitcoin locked, your ZEC sealed, as before.

10
00:00:56,970 --> 00:01:00,044
Then silence. The claim never comes.

11
00:01:00,044 --> 00:01:08,044
The deadline passes and the swap is called off. Both ways back were signed in advance.

12
00:01:08,044 --> 00:01:18,045
Your ZEC comes back to your shielded wallet. Nobody lost a coin.

13
00:01:18,045 --> 00:01:25,050
No custodian. ZEC never leaves the shielded pool. And when it succeeds, the Bitcoin side looks like any other payment.
